  "slug": "microscopy-ctspyderfields-f439",
  "summary": "Model structure must be checked against contact surveys and herd or contact-tree data before interpreting transmission model outputs.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether empirical contact and transmission data can validate the structure of mechanistic models before their outputs are interpreted. An agent-based model with random-mixing contacts matches survey contacts in households, schools and workplaces but misses age-structured contacts in outside settings, a structural discrepancy relevant to model selection. A multi-type branching likelihood for contact trees is validated on simulations and COVID-19 data from Karnataka, while a particle-MCMC analysis of Salmonella Dublin in Öland cattle attributes roughly half of the force of infection to local spread and half to within-herd transmission, with Rt approximately 1. The items address distinct problem classes, so this thread is not uniform across them. What is new is the use of contact-level or herd-level validation to test model adequacy prior to inference on outputs.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-06-23"
}
